00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i  am indeed here to talk to you um about  whiteness but maybe i should tell you  a little bit about myself first and why  i want to talk to you about whiteness  and first off um as i was introduced my  name is dr miriam fraser i'm a political  scientist by training with a phd  from oxford examining postcolonial  resistance  to western hegemony in north africa  and these days i work primarily as a  journalist and a documentary filmmaker  now just over a year ago i started a  podcast called we need  to talk about whiteness for those of you  who would like to listen to that you can  catch it on  spotify and itunes and the objective  really behind the podcast  was to explore the meaning of whiteness  why did i want to do that  well largely for personal reasons to be  honest  um i became increasingly aware in my  personal life that many of my friends  and loved ones  from non-white backgrounds were sharing  a sort of bubbling  frustration with the lack of racial  awareness  arrogance and entitlement they  frequently encountered in their day to  day  were still in many of those instances  other people  other white people were present in  witnessing these instances  but often stayed quiet like many of us  might do  you witness something that somebody else  does it feels uncomfortable  it looks wrong but you didn't do it so  it really doesn't feel like you have to  speak up  only it became increasingly obvious to  me that that's exactly what needed to  happen  that we could no longer leave the burden  of discussing  racism or racial injustices or calling  out racial injustices  upon the victim of those injustices and  in fact  those of us who were witnesses in many  ways  could end up looking like all being  silently complicit  if we didn't take a stand so  um some of these instances i thought i  would share with you  um to give you a sense of the sort of  deep frustration  i was experiencing listening to them one  was a very senior  black academic walking into her first  day in a  very prestigious new job at a big um  academic institution  and being instructed as she walked  through the doors to change the bins  please before she left  um because she was mistaken for the  cleaning lady  um and another occasion an asian film  producer friend of mine had been told  by a very senior film industry executive  that she should count herself lucky to  be at the table  and that she hadn't been on a killed yet  and that was received with rapturous uh  laughter  by the rest of the table and so what i  was hearing increasingly from my friends  that they  was that they were telling me they had  to hide or even shield  who they are in their workplace where  their racial and cultural identity  singled them out for differential  treatment the question was  and is different from what um  and as i delved a little deeper i came  across a term which first developed  um in critical race studies in america  whiteness and whiteness studies as  a whole field uh which grew out of um  studies uh in the u.s um so if you're  interested in learning more about that i  would look into  whiteness studies in america now what  does whitening studies do  they look at the historical and social  formation of white identity  i began reading up on it and realizing  that some of my favorite writers from  bell hooks to  james baldwin had in fact written about  whiteness and describing it in bell  hooks's words for example  as terrorism in reference to the fear  she experienced as a child  walking through a white neighborhood i'd  never really heard white  identity described in those terms in  fact what i realized was i'd never  really thought about  white identity i'd never really given  much thought to what it meant to be  racialized as  white because i'd largely assumed it was  a  neutral and meaningless identity  and what i realized was of course that  um  it had that whiteness is so normative  that it's so  um uh so deeply embedded  in our society that we uh as white  people are  rarely aware of its significance and  particularly if we're not listening to  how whiteness is experienced by those  who are excluded to it  what that meant is i felt uh the need to  confront that meaning to understand what  white racial identity means to  those excluded from it and to try and  understand that  as a signifier that i was carrying  unwittingly in my day to day  with clear implications for my  interactions but which i was largely and  remain in many ways  largely unaware of even as i talked to  you now  now one of the best uh quotes in this  area which i i  always refer to is david wallace foster  who refers to  the fish in water you know i describe uh  whiteness as the water you know one fish  is swimming along  um and crosses another fish and says uh  hey  uh hey how's the water today and the  other fish says  what water and that's basically how i  conceptualize whiteness in my days today  we're all swimming in it  um some of us are aware that it's there  and others um are  so uh used to it that we that we never  really give it another thought because  we're swimming with the current and it's  never  swimming against us now um  what i also came to realize that whilst  a lot of interrogation was being  placed upon my black or asian friends so  you know what's it like to be black or  how many times have i heard someone say  to one of my friends what's it like to  be an asian woman  uh and yeah i realize we so rarely  interrogate the meaning of white racial  identity so i started to get into a  habit of trying to ask people  you know so what's it like being white  um  not in a uh flippant way not um in a way  that was deliberately trying to be  confrontational but really trying to  assess  the you know people's reactions when you  start to ask that question  um and so my starting point really for  the podcast and for the website was to  explore the meaning of whiteness both as  it's conceptualized as those who are  racialized as white and those who are  not  now caveat and quite an important one um  is uh the idea that race of course is a  construct  there's no uh biological scientific uh  rootedness to  uh race race is a theory that was  developed  um to justify uh what i call race a  category of power and what i mean by  that is that it emerged  uh in a context in which uh different  groups needed to be  divided in order to advance the  interests of  what you know what was at that time the  one percent were probably still the one  percent  um and so uh in that sense uh although  race is a construct racism which is the  uh outcome  of that construct that was created to  justify  hierarchies of human value and then to  justify things like slavery and  colonialism and imperialism  and unequal treatment of people more  broadly in society along racial lines  um that continues to have a very real  world impact on people's lives even to  this day  um so what is whiteness well you'll find  a few definitions online if you want  i think of whiteness as an  interconnected system of power which  come  which from the personal to the political  asserts the supremacy  of white western europeans their ideas  structures  and actions and in so doing forms what i  refer to as a form of cultural myopia  which in discounting the value of  non-european output  is ultimately limiting self-serving and  harmful  to those excluded from those narrow from  this narrow perception of both the self  and the world so i think um whiteness as  a structure is harmful to everybody but  it's particularly harmful  to those who are excluded uh from it um  why me um well uh to those who might be  wondering what my racial identity is  um i'm half irish half french meaning i  am of european  heritage uh my religion is that i am a  muslim  and for most of my adult life i wore  a headscarf meaning that i was  identified  um as muslim in a way that perhaps  you wouldn't recognize it looking at me  now so  why did i want to begin this  conversation well um first off for  a very simple reason which is i believe  change has to start at home  within ourselves and this is an issue  that i have watched uh close friends and  loved ones grapple with  um for decades and it wasn't really  tenable for me uh to sit on the  sidelines and sort of  bemoan the situation or sort of say well  you know i'm not like that so it's fine  i sort of felt like well actually  um i may not hold those views or at  least i sort of don't think i do  but i'm sort of not actively um  challenging them  in my environment and that for me didn't  feel like a  um tenable long-term situation so i felt  a strong moral obligation to get in  on the pitch um and there's also another  more personal reason which is um i  suppose that as a muslim woman  who wore a headscarf for much of my  adult life i feel like i have  experienced  whiteness uh first hand meaning that um  when i wore herself more consistently i  was  regularly racialized as um excluded from  whiteness as non-white  um white uh people who uh identify as  white or identified as white would  frequently um  assume that i was uh not uh of the same  racial category and  um that led me to a whole load of um  interesting  uh and and quite worrying experiences  over the course of my life  um so what did it feel like what does it  feel like being um  excluded from whiteness and then um you  know having  the uh quote-unquote privilege of  slipping back into it well  what being excluded from whiteness felt  like to me was  like being an outsider and it felt like  being viewed with suspicion walking into  a shop having a security guard  follow me around it felt like having a  question mark having  uh hanging over my worth which needed to  be  proven in every interaction and  conversation whereas  others other people's worth was simply a  given  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

